    "description": "Social support in Iran includes household cash transfers, targeted assistance and services for people facing deprivation. Its effectiveness depends on purchasing power, identification of need and access to complementary services.",
    "tags": ["Social assistance", "Cash transfers", "Child poverty", "Welfare services", "Social inclusion"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:32:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:32:20Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Support in Iran: Cash Assistance and Welfare Services",
      "description": "Learn how household transfers, targeted welfare services and child-sensitive support fit together in Iran’s social assistance landscape."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "support-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Beyond employment-related insurance",
        "content": "Social support addresses needs that cannot be understood solely through a person’s employment insurance. In Iran, it includes broad household transfers alongside more targeted cash, in-kind assistance and social services. Research by the International Policy Centre for Inclusive Growth identifies the State Welfare Organization and the Imam Khomeini Relief Foundation as major assistance providers, with provincial and local structures involved in implementation. [s1]\n\nThe same research describes the expansion of household cash compensation during subsidy reform. Although targeting poorer households was an initial objective, administrative difficulties helped produce very broad coverage. This history explains why a payment associated with welfare is not necessarily evidence that its recipient has been assessed as poor. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "purchasing-power",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Payments and purchasing power",
        "content": "The World Bank’s Iran Economic Monitor describes how inflation weakened the real value of regular cash transfers and how additional payments responded to subsidy changes and economic shocks. Its household-survey analysis distinguishes regular transfers from a separate category combining temporary payments, scholarships and charity. That mixed category cannot be read as the spending or reach of a single programme. [s2]\n\nFor the period 2020/21–2022/23, the report finds that poorer households relied more on cash transfers, whereas better-off households relied more on pensions. It attributes an important part of the later poverty reduction to expanded temporary transfers. These findings concern the reporting periods studied; they do not establish current payment amounts or prove that every eligible household received adequate support. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cash-plus",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Combining money with services",
        "content": "Cash-plus approaches combine financial assistance with services intended to address barriers that money alone may not remove. A UNICEF-hosted evaluability assessment describes interventions involving education, health and nutrition support, counselling, parental employability and vocational skills. Its distinction between programme design and demonstrated results is essential: an evaluability assessment examines whether an intervention is sufficiently defined and measurable for evaluation; it is not proof that the intended long-term effects have occurred. [s3]\n\nUNICEF’s Iran programme also emphasizes identifying vulnerable households, mapping the needs of children with disabilities and improving monitoring. Its account notes particular disadvantages faced by rural girls and identifies data, targeting and legislative weaknesses. National coverage totals therefore need to be read alongside evidence about who is missed and whether suitable services are actually accessible. [s4]\n\nThe practical question is broader than whether a household receives a transfer. Social support must also be assessed against the needs it seeks to address: food security, education, health, disability inclusion and protection from deprivation. UNICEF presents these as connected policy objectives, rather than interchangeable outcomes established by a payment record. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
